WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:05.660
Imagine if every mistake you ever made in math class wasn't just erased, but it was like

00:05.660 --> 00:09.820
permanently etched into the fabric of the universe as a foundational law.

00:10.180 --> 00:11.920
Oh man, that sounds like an absolute nightmare.

00:12.160 --> 00:15.640
Right. I mean, you write the wrong number, you realize it, you try to scratch it out,

00:15.700 --> 00:19.440
but the scratch out itself becomes this permanent mathematical object.

00:19.560 --> 00:20.500
You can't escape it.

00:20.600 --> 00:26.660
Exactly. Well, today we are exploring a radical new programming and mathematical framework that

00:26.660 --> 00:33.460
operates on exactly that premise. We're looking at a universe where there are quite literally no

00:33.460 --> 00:34.600
erasers. Not at all.

00:34.860 --> 00:42.820
Welcome to the deep dive. Our sources today are two incredibly dense, but fascinating foundational

00:42.820 --> 00:49.640
papers. One is called dependent-type-theory.pdf and the other is refusal-structures.pdf.

00:49.740 --> 00:50.920
Very light reading, you know.

00:51.000 --> 00:56.400
Oh, total beach reads. But our mission today is to figure out what happens when we stop

00:56.400 --> 01:00.740
treating computer programs and equations as things we can just constantly overwrite and

01:00.740 --> 01:03.180
start treating them as permanent, irreversible histories.

01:03.700 --> 01:08.960
Yeah. And it completely upends our fundamental relationship with computation. I mean, for decades,

01:09.140 --> 01:13.180
we have treated computers essentially as incredibly fast chalkboards.

01:13.260 --> 01:14.140
Okay, chalkboards.

01:14.280 --> 01:18.540
You know, you write an equation, solve a step, wipe away the old numbers, and just write the

01:18.540 --> 01:22.220
new ones right over them. We prioritize the current state, like the present moment.

01:22.360 --> 01:23.000
The right now.

01:23.000 --> 01:29.000
Exactly. But in this new framework, which is called SpherePop, that chalkboard is just

01:29.000 --> 01:34.900
thrown out the window. We are suddenly dealing with a bound ledger written in pen.

01:35.640 --> 01:37.340
SpherePop. I love that name, by the way.

01:37.340 --> 01:43.040
It's a great name. But in SpherePop, every single step, every choice, and every abandoned

01:43.040 --> 01:50.520
path is structurally recorded. And that historical ledger actually becomes the primitive building

01:50.520 --> 01:52.300
block of mathematical identity itself.

01:52.400 --> 01:55.740
Okay, let's unpack this, because my immediate reaction is just, well, why?

01:55.880 --> 01:56.900
Right. It sounds exhausting.

01:57.020 --> 02:01.800
Yeah. Why would we ever want that? Overriding memory is efficient. If I have a variable in

02:01.800 --> 02:07.400
a program, let's say, x equals 5, and later I update it to x equals 10, the 5 is gone.

02:07.680 --> 02:12.660
Right. The machine only knows the 10. That seems clean to me. It keeps things fast and simple

02:12.660 --> 02:13.680
for the computer, right?

02:13.680 --> 02:19.240
Well, it seems clean until you have a bug or, you know, security flaw or an AI system

02:19.240 --> 02:21.360
that spits out a catastrophic hallucination.

02:21.440 --> 02:22.380
Oh, yeah. True.

02:22.520 --> 02:25.780
And then you have absolutely no way of proving how it arrived at that state.

02:26.500 --> 02:31.500
Classical math and programming rely on what you just described, state transformation.

02:31.780 --> 02:32.820
Overriding the past.

02:32.880 --> 02:38.560
Exactly. You overwrite the past to get to the present. Or, in classical set theory, identity

02:38.560 --> 02:45.440
relies on static membership. An object just is what it is. But SpherePop argues that this

02:45.440 --> 02:47.260
amnesia is our biggest vulnerability.

02:48.100 --> 02:49.980
Amnesia. That's a really good way to put it.

02:50.200 --> 02:55.220
In this new framework, every meaningful object possesses what the authors call provenance,

02:55.580 --> 02:56.100
a history.

02:56.280 --> 02:57.660
So it remembers where it came from.

02:57.840 --> 03:02.500
Yes. A program is no longer just a machine that takes an input and spits out an output.

03:02.500 --> 03:07.800
It is the accumulated record of the specific events that actually brought it into existence.

03:07.800 --> 03:13.780
So you were saying sequential order matters intrinsically. Like, event A followed by event

03:13.780 --> 03:18.660
B is a fundamentally different creature than event B followed by event A.

03:18.740 --> 03:19.020
Yes.

03:19.140 --> 03:23.640
Unless the system can mathematically prove they are completely independent of each other?

03:23.860 --> 03:30.660
That is the core of it, yeah. Conventional theories often treat contexts as, like, unordered

03:30.660 --> 03:36.100
bags of assumptions. You just toss your variables into a pile and pull them out when you need

03:36.100 --> 03:36.280
them.

03:36.280 --> 03:38.160
Like reaching into a grab bag.

03:38.300 --> 03:45.860
Exactly. But SpherePop enforces a strict directed arrow of time. If you declare a variable and

03:45.860 --> 03:50.140
then use it, your final result carries the exact lineage of how it was made.

03:50.280 --> 03:54.300
But, I mean, my mind immediately goes to the sheer scale of the data.

03:54.420 --> 03:55.480
Oh, the storage problem.

03:55.580 --> 04:00.580
Right. If we never ever delete anything, doesn't this history get overwhelmingly huge? I mean,

04:00.580 --> 04:04.460
for you listening, think about your laptop running a background loop a million times a second.

04:04.460 --> 04:05.600
Yeah, it sounds impossible.

04:05.980 --> 04:10.720
If it never overwrites a single piece of memory, doesn't the entire system just grind to a halt

04:10.720 --> 04:12.120
from the weight of its own history?

04:12.420 --> 04:17.740
It would, yeah. But the authors actually anticipated that exact bottleneck. They solve it through a

04:17.740 --> 04:19.520
mechanism called historical compression.

04:19.840 --> 04:22.000
Okay. Historical compression. How does that work?

04:22.280 --> 04:24.900
Well, because these histories are strictly immutable.

04:25.320 --> 04:28.260
Meaning they physically cannot be changed once, written right now.

04:28.260 --> 04:33.620
Exactly. Because they are immutable, the system doesn't actually store duplicate-proof trees or

04:33.620 --> 04:35.300
repeated paths over and over again.

04:35.820 --> 04:40.460
I might need a visual for how that actually compresses data. If I run a loop a million

04:40.460 --> 04:43.540
times, how is that not a million separate histories to store?

04:43.960 --> 04:49.820
Okay. Think of a massive tree with a single trunk that splits into thousands of branches.

04:49.820 --> 04:55.100
If you have a sprawling history of a thousand events, and two different computations branch

04:55.100 --> 04:57.180
off from, let's say, event 500.

04:57.700 --> 04:58.960
Okay. I'm picturing it.

04:59.300 --> 05:04.800
The system only stores those first 500 events once. It uses what they call shared prefixes.

05:05.440 --> 05:09.580
Both of those new branches just point back to that single shared historical trunk.

05:09.920 --> 05:10.720
Oh, wow.

05:10.840 --> 05:15.160
Yeah. The source text describes this as turning an infinite sprawling forest of redundant data

05:15.160 --> 05:19.520
into a tightly woven directed acyclic graph.

05:19.820 --> 05:24.100
Wait, hold on. Directed acyclic graph. I know what a graph is, like a network of connected points.

05:24.800 --> 05:28.780
But break down directed acyclic in the context of this history for us.

05:28.860 --> 05:32.180
Sure. So, directed means the connections only go one way.

05:32.260 --> 05:33.080
Like a one-way street.

05:33.220 --> 05:38.700
Exactly. The arrow of time always points forward. And acyclic means there are no cycles.

05:38.820 --> 05:43.260
You can never loop back and point to your own future or alter your own past.

05:43.740 --> 05:44.940
No time travel allowed.

05:45.160 --> 05:45.800
No time travel.

05:46.020 --> 05:46.200
Yeah.

05:46.280 --> 05:52.260
So, instead of storing massive blocks of identical data, the computer is just storing tiny mathematical

05:52.260 --> 05:56.700
pointers indicating where a new event branches off from the established timeline.

05:56.980 --> 05:59.660
It basically compresses the history structurally.

05:59.660 --> 06:04.680
Okay. So, if everything in this universe is built from this immutable acyclic history,

06:04.880 --> 06:07.680
I'm trying to picture the actual mechanics of writing it.

06:07.740 --> 06:11.000
Like, how does a computer actually take a step forward in SpherePop?

06:11.000 --> 06:16.680
Well, the author breaks down the entire kernel of this system into just five primitive operations.

06:17.040 --> 06:19.680
Five structural verbs, essentially, that build the timeline.

06:19.840 --> 06:20.920
Five verbs. Got it.

06:21.040 --> 06:22.140
The first one is pop.

06:22.640 --> 06:25.820
Pop! That sounds almost, I don't know, casual.

06:25.820 --> 06:28.500
It does, but it's the act of commitment.

06:29.460 --> 06:29.720
Pop!

06:29.720 --> 06:30.360
Pop!

06:30.360 --> 06:30.720
Pop!

06:30.720 --> 06:30.760
Pop!

06:30.760 --> 06:31.280
Pop!

06:31.280 --> 06:31.360
Pop!

06:31.360 --> 06:31.760
Pop!

06:31.760 --> 06:32.360
Pop!

06:32.360 --> 06:36.300
A specific branch or continuation was chosen out of several possibilities.

06:36.400 --> 06:38.100
Like arriving at a fork in the road.

06:38.180 --> 06:42.780
Yes. You arrive at a fork in the road, and you permanently log the decision to take the

06:42.780 --> 06:43.440
left path.

06:43.800 --> 06:47.960
Which brings up the obvious question of, uh, what happens to the right path.

06:48.020 --> 06:52.200
Ah, that is handled by the second primitive operation, which is refuse.

06:52.560 --> 06:53.000
Refuse.

06:53.060 --> 06:56.900
This permanently records the intentional rejection of an alternative.

06:56.900 --> 07:01.740
I love this concept, but I want to make sure I'm visualizing it correctly. Because in normal

07:01.740 --> 07:07.300
programming, if a condition is false, like an if statement that fails, the program just

07:07.300 --> 07:08.800
silently ignores it and moves on.

07:08.860 --> 07:09.580
Right, it just drops it.

07:09.600 --> 07:13.360
The rejected path just vanishes into the ether. But here, you're saying?

07:13.680 --> 07:18.640
What's fascinating here is that traditional systems discard failure as useless noise.

07:19.620 --> 07:23.300
SpherePop elevates rejection to a foundational building block.

07:23.360 --> 07:23.820
Wow.

07:23.820 --> 07:29.640
Making refuse a primitive operation means the rejected alternatives actually carry mathematical

07:29.640 --> 07:30.060
weight.

07:30.320 --> 07:36.180
So it's like Robert Frost's The Road Not Taken. Pop is the act of walking down the chosen path,

07:36.380 --> 07:42.540
but refuse is literally building a permanent brick wall across the other path so future historians

07:42.540 --> 07:44.380
know you actively rejected it.

07:44.680 --> 07:50.680
That is a brilliant way to look at it. Knowing what you cannot do shapes the geometry of what

07:50.680 --> 07:51.140
you can do.

07:51.140 --> 07:56.800
It's like playing a hyper-strict game of chess. If you touch a piece, realize moving it would put

07:56.800 --> 08:01.120
your king in check, and decide not to move it. You don't just take the move back.

08:01.240 --> 08:01.420
Right.

08:01.740 --> 08:05.520
The system permanently etches a rule onto the board that says,

08:05.640 --> 08:09.960
this specific move was considered and mathematically rejected here.

08:10.320 --> 08:15.920
Yes, exactly. And your opponent, or in this case the computer, can use that newly etched rule

08:15.920 --> 08:21.480
to understand the boundaries of the game better. For future optimization, or for an AI trying to

08:21.480 --> 08:27.420
verify its logic, knowing a path was explicitly proven to be a dead end is incredibly valuable.

08:27.560 --> 08:28.500
It saves time later.

08:28.680 --> 08:32.000
Right. It prevents the system from ever needing to explore that dead end again.

08:32.240 --> 08:37.860
Okay. So we have choosing a path with pop and blocking a path with refuse. But how does these

08:37.860 --> 08:44.300
choices actually stick together? Like, if I take a step today, how does the system know it relies on a

08:44.300 --> 08:45.380
step I took yesterday?

08:45.700 --> 08:47.760
That is the third verb, bind.

08:48.020 --> 08:48.580
Bind.

08:48.780 --> 08:54.520
Bind is the dependency tracker. It is the system permanently recording that one future construction

08:54.520 --> 08:59.960
historically relies on a previous one. It mathematically glues the timeline together.

08:59.960 --> 09:02.320
So you can't just pull things out of thin air.

09:02.460 --> 09:07.840
Exactly. It ensures that you cannot somehow extract a conclusion from the ledger without

09:07.840 --> 09:10.980
dragging its entire prerequisite history along with it.

09:11.300 --> 09:15.280
Okay. I'm following the mechanics. We are building this massive web of history using pop,

09:15.440 --> 09:20.240
refuse, and bind. But if we are constantly generating all this history and we can never

09:20.240 --> 09:24.620
erase anything, what stops the system from filling up with absolute garbage?

09:24.940 --> 09:26.300
That is the big question.

09:26.300 --> 09:31.540
Right. Because if I try to run a broken piece of code or divide by zero, I don't want that

09:31.540 --> 09:33.820
permanent mistake corrupting the pristine timeline.

09:34.020 --> 09:38.340
Well, this brings us to the second paper in our sources, which focuses entirely on refusal

09:38.340 --> 09:43.420
structures. This is where we have to fundamentally redefine what a type is.

09:43.560 --> 09:48.580
Wait, like data types? Integers, strings of text, booleans, that kind of thing?

09:48.680 --> 09:54.360
Yes, exactly. In traditional programming, types are just static bins that hold data. You know,

09:54.360 --> 09:56.480
the system checks if you put a number in the number bin.

09:56.600 --> 09:58.440
And if you did, great. You're good to go.

09:58.600 --> 10:04.860
Right. But in sphere pop, a type is not a bin. A type is a disciplined refusal structure.

10:05.280 --> 10:11.660
A disciplined refusal structure. Okay. I am picturing a highly trained bouncer at an exclusive

10:11.660 --> 10:12.300
nightclub.

10:12.520 --> 10:13.000
I like that.

10:13.180 --> 10:17.940
In a normal system, the bouncer just glances at your ID at the door, right? Like static checking.

10:17.940 --> 10:21.380
But the sphere pop bouncer is actively managing the timeline.

10:21.920 --> 10:26.920
And that bouncer has three possible responses to any historical event trying to enter the club.

10:27.080 --> 10:27.220
Yeah.

10:27.400 --> 10:28.540
The first is admit.

10:28.720 --> 10:29.760
Meaning you're on the list.

10:29.940 --> 10:34.620
Exactly. This means the history is coherent. It follows the rules, the dependencies check

