The second response has to be refuse.

10:38.160 --> 10:43.740
Correct. Refuse means the construction is malformed. You are kicked out.

10:43.740 --> 10:50.760
The bouncer prevents a logically broken piece of code from ever becoming an executable commitment

10:50.760 --> 10:51.560
in the history.

10:52.120 --> 10:56.900
So far, that just sounds like a standard compiler throwing an error. But there's a third response

10:56.900 --> 10:59.760
the bouncer can give, right? The peeper calls it defer.

11:00.080 --> 11:04.680
Defer is the absolute brilliance of the system. The bouncer essentially says, hey, wait right

11:04.680 --> 11:08.820
here. I don't have enough information to let you in, but I'm not rejecting you yet.

11:09.020 --> 11:09.800
Oh, that's interesting.

11:09.800 --> 11:15.060
It allows a piece of code to say, I am missing a variable, or I don't have all the answers

11:15.060 --> 11:16.900
yet, but keep this history open.

11:17.040 --> 11:20.920
But why is keeping it open so important? Why not just kick them out and make the programmer

