massive philosophical and mathematical problem.

13:05.980 --> 13:06.280
It does.

13:06.400 --> 13:08.020
Here's where it gets really interesting.

13:09.120 --> 13:17.280
If every single object's identity is tied strictly to its unique individual history, how can two different

13:17.280 --> 13:19.380
things ever be considered equal?

13:19.380 --> 13:21.340
Ah, the equality problem.

13:21.380 --> 13:23.080
Let me pose a simple scenario for you.

13:23.620 --> 13:25.220
I compute two plus two.

13:25.340 --> 13:26.060
It equals four.

13:26.320 --> 13:27.740
You compute three plus one.

13:27.880 --> 13:29.160
It also equals four.

13:29.280 --> 13:32.700
In classical math, we say those are just equal four is four.

13:32.920 --> 13:34.860
We just look at the observable result.

13:34.940 --> 13:35.220
Right.

13:35.680 --> 13:41.220
But SpherePop forces us to separate observable equality from historical identity.

13:41.880 --> 13:47.080
Two plus two and three plus one might compute the exact same observable result, but they took

13:47.080 --> 13:48.840
completely different paths to get there.

13:48.920 --> 13:52.680
They have entirely different constructive histories, so they aren't historically identical.

13:53.080 --> 13:54.200
But again, I have to push back.

13:54.640 --> 13:59.060
Isn't it exhausting for a computer to constantly track the difference between the four that came

